A firewall filter implements a 'default deny' policy by first defining explicit 'permit' rules for necessary traffic based on criteria like IP addresses, ports, and protocols, followed by a final 'deny all' rule to block any traffic not matched by the preceding permit rules. For example, in a Juniper SRX firewall, this would look like:
```juniper
security {
    policies {
        from-zone untrust to-zone trust {
            policy allow-web {
                match {
                    source-address any;
                    destination-address any;
                    application junos-defaults {
                        web-browsing;
                    }
                }
                then {
                    permit;
                }
            }
            policy deny-all {
                match {
                    source-address any;
                    destination-address any;
                    application any;
                }
                then {
                    deny;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This configuration first permits web browsing traffic and then explicitly denies all other traffic.

